  "lineage": "Quechua-and-Aymara Andean dance-song tradition with anhemitonic-pentatonic five-tone (no half-step) melodic-vocabulary and characteristic short-short-long iambic-dance pattern (Q-Q-half rhythm) over moderate-fast 2/4 metric pulse. Pre-Columbian-origin (likely Inca-Imperial 1438-1533 onward, though oral-tradition extends earlier) with post-colonial mestizaje fusion (Spanish-criollo) integration of guitar, harp, charango ten-string-small-armadillo-back-guitar, and bandurria. Regional variants span Cuzco huayno-cusqueño (slower-court-elevated), Ayacucho huayno-ayacuchano (faster-rural), Junín huayno-orquestino (brass-band-and-saxophone modernized), and Aymara huayño-altiplánico (Bolivian-highland variant). The canonical instrumental ensemble includes quena vertical-notch-flute and zampoña panpipes.",
